the race Solid or Safe Republican", "Polymarket prices Gooden at about 97%", "House Ethics investigation is the only negative headline, with no polling showing impact"], "rationale": "A Republican-leaning district, a renominated incumbent with a roughly hundred-to-one cash advantage, unanimous Solid or Safe Republican ratings from all ten raters, and market pricing near 97 percent for Gooden all point the same way; the July ethics inquiry and a favorable Democratic environment are not enough to move a race this structurally lopsided.", "watch": "A formal ethics finding against Gooden or an unexpectedly large Democratic midterm wave could tighten the margin, though neither appears positioned to threaten the seat.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Lance Gooden (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Texas' 5th District, in the Dallas area, leans Republican by about ten points. Lance Gooden, the Republican incumbent, was renominated and enters the general election with a large financial edge, having raised roughly $1.3 million, while Democratic nominee Chelsey Hockett has raised about $13,000 and reports only around $5,000 on hand. No outside spending is directed against Gooden.\n\nEvery major race rating places the district in Solid or Safe Republican territory, and prediction markets give Gooden about a 97 percent chance of winning. The main friction point is a House Ethics Committee investigation reported in July, but no polling indicates it has moved the race, and the district's fundamentals dominate. A Democratic-leaning national environment gives Hockett her best opening, yet the structural advantages remain firmly with Gooden.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0352, "margin": -14.68}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0318, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0406, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0384, "margin": -14.0}, {"